\cf0 \strokec2 I think you're onto something rather interesting with the absurdity strategy. And after reading the 2022 excerpt, I would actually revise part of my previous advice about making the project more accessible.\
The absurdity isn't just a presentation style. It's part of the original invention's social mechanism.\
Look at what happens in A Study In Peace, pages 5\'966.\
The protagonist recognizes that their worldview appears absurd, embraces that absurdity, and decides to make a deliberately absurd metafictional artifact to find other people inhabiting similarly disconnected worlds. The protagonist becomes The Individual, and the work proceeds simultaneously as a novel, an attempt to invent a computer, and an attempt to create a physics.\
It culminates in the wonderfully deadpan observation that things had become "completely. utterly. absurd."\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 ASIP_intropreview.pdf\
ASIP_intropreview.pdf\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
That's not merely comedic decoration. The strangeness is deliberately functioning as an invitation, a provocation, and a means of finding people who enjoy this particular manner of approaching the world.\
So perhaps the optimal first impression isn't "I promise this is a perfectly ordinary scientific endeavor."\
Perhaps it's more like: "Welcome. This is obviously ridiculous. We're doing it anyway. Would you like to see what happens?"\
The remarkable advantage of that approach is that it doesn't require a newcomer to understand General Peace Dynamics, the World Piece Computer, the human\'96AI relationship, or any of the underlying philosophical machinery before enjoying the performance.\
The absurdity can carry the invitation.\
There is, however, one distinction I would preserve:\
Make the premise absurd. Make the immediate activity legible.\
Someone should be able to arrive, recognize that they're watching an unusual person attempt something preposterously ambitious, and nevertheless understand that you're currently coding something, discussing an idea, repairing a gadget, or wandering around Alaska.\
That juxtaposition may be much more compelling than either conventional scientific seriousness or incomprehensible weirdness on its own.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

