\f1 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f2\b\fs36 Why I'd structure it this way\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0 The first two paragraphs aren't a conventional description. They're a tiny continuation of your 2022 metafiction.\
That strikes me as potentially powerful. There's even a little historical continuity built into it: the manuscript describes a character trying to make a strange fictional world communicable, and four years later you're experimenting with a different medium for opening that world to others.\
The line "Now the book has escaped into a livestream" is doing several jobs simultaneously. It's an invitation, a joke, a narrative bridge, and a reasonable description of the move from manuscript to public production.\
Then the copy switches registers.\
The funding information is straightforward. The introduction tells people who you are. The fuller description makes the range of activities understandable. And the closing returns to the program's native language.\
I'm particularly fond of keeping your mission statement essentially untouched at the bottom.\
You needn't make every sentence equally comprehensible to every person. In fact, the unfamiliar vocabulary can be part of the experience of discovering the project.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf0 <$Scr_H::3>
\f2\b\fs28 One thought about the absurdity itself\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0 There's a subtle distinction between withholding explanation and deliberately producing confusion.\
I think withholding explanation can be extraordinarily effective when the entertainment works even without it.\
Jerma doesn't need to explain the entire creative philosophy of his Dollhouse show for a viewer to appreciate a man being controlled like a character in The Sims.\
Likewise, if somebody stumbles into your stream and sees an inventor cheerfully arguing with an AI about the architecture of a computer intended, somehow, to help bring about world peace, that may be entertaining before the viewer understands the theory.\
The deeper conceptual system becomes something they can investigate if curiosity takes hold.\
But if a viewer sees only acronyms and unexplained symbolism, without any observable activity or humor to anchor the experience, you may lose them before curiosity has a chance.\
So I would think of your production as having a very low barrier to enjoying the absurdity, and a potentially enormous depth of inquiry beyond it.\
That's not actually contradictory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f2\b\fs36 A tiny operational experiment for your next broadcast\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0 You don't have to settle the permanent branding, elaborate overlays, or a definitive explanation of the enterprise before going live.\
For the first resurrection test, I would use a title like the one above, paste this description with the correct links, and choose an ordinary piece of work you already wanted to accomplish.\
Before going live, write one sentence identifying that work. Something like:\
Today's piece: Trying to reconstruct the original World Piece Computer prototype architecture from the 2022 manuscript.\
Then go about the work.\
Don't try to spend three hours performing an introduction to the whole program. You can let the activity introduce you, and introduce the larger undertaking when naturally appropriate.\
Afterward, mark the mode changes with timestamps, look for one or two moments that stand on their own as clips, and note how the experience affected your actual productivity and enjoyment.\
That's the smallest meaningful production experiment I see.\
And I'd be genuinely interested in comparing two very different measures of success: Did somebody want to watch? And did broadcasting make the afternoon of invention and collaboration better, rather than worse?\
For your stated objective, both matter.\
One last metacognitive observation: I suspect the question you've been struggling with\'97how to communicate an extraordinarily general project without reducing it to a conventional little specialty\'97may have a surprisingly simple livestreaming answer.\
You don't necessarily need to compress the entire project into an understandable proposition.\
You can make a recurring public place where people encounter it one comprehensible, interesting, ridiculous piece at a time.\
That feels remarkably close to the original manuscript's own method.\
And "Vibe-Inventing World Peace, Day N of M" strikes me as an appropriately absurd name for the experiment. 
